00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.520
hello and welcome i'm james murphy from mCoding&nbsp;
and today we're going to be talking about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.040
def main if name main idiom in python and why&nbsp;
you should be using it in all of your scripts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.200
first off not every file is a script by a&nbsp;
script i mean a file that you intend to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.560
not a library so something like this&nbsp;
that just defines a function that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.800
you're supposed to import in another file&nbsp;
is not going to benefit from this idiom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:32.800
most languages like c++ or java have a kind&nbsp;
of boilerplate setup that you need to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.280
even to write the most basic function java&nbsp;
is notorious for having its public static&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.000
void main string args as one of the first things&nbsp;
that a programmer sees before learning to use it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.120
but in python it's seemingly a lot simpler&nbsp;
all you need to do is say print hello world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.920
but what i hope to convince you of by the end&nbsp;
of this video is that you should really prefer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:58.720
this def main if name main idiom over just writing&nbsp;
things out in a line okay really quickly if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.960
not familiar what is this name variable if you&nbsp;
run the script from your terminal or elsewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:10.000
you'll see that the name variable turns out to&nbsp;
be this double underscore main double underscore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.160
but if you import the file&nbsp;
you see something different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.800
in this case we see the double underscore name is&nbsp;
if name main package dot import me to print name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.520
the difference in behavior allows you to check&nbsp;
whether the file is being run as a script or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.880
if you see dunder main then it's being run as&nbsp;
a script and if it's anything else then it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.960
being imported you could just stop there and put&nbsp;
all the statements that you want to execute when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:44.560
this is run as a script into this if block&nbsp;
but i say you should go one step further and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.880
define it as a main function okay so you&nbsp;
can check whether or not your code is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.760
being run as a script or being imported&nbsp;
but why why would you want to do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.960
is it just a way to flex on programmers that&nbsp;
don't know the idiom or is there a reason for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.440
actually doing this well yes of course there&nbsp;
are reasons for doing it let's go over them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.360
the first and most important reason actually&nbsp;
has nothing to do with the language itself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.960
everything to do with how people use it yes you&nbsp;
can use if name main to check whether or not your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:23.200
code is being run versus imported but this isn't&nbsp;
what it signals to the person reading your code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.360
if name main is used to signal that&nbsp;
this file is a script and you can run it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:37.200
if i look at a file and i don't see if name main&nbsp;
i assume that i cannot or should not try to run it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.440
it makes me think it's supposed to be a&nbsp;
library or that it's only meant to be imported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.120
nothing about the python language actually&nbsp;
enforces this but compare it to using an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.760
underscore as a variable name if you see&nbsp;
an underscore in someone's code they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:57.360
basically saying i'm not going to use this&nbsp;
variable nothing stops them from using it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:04.240
but if they do use it they're kind of breaking&nbsp;
an unspoken rule in the python community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:10.160
underscore means i'm not going to use this&nbsp;
variable and if name main means this is a script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.960
don't keep your reader guessing if this is&nbsp;
supposed to be an entry point to your program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:21.040
just make it explicit many editors like pycharm&nbsp;
use this as a signal you can see the file on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.000
the left has this green arrow which says run&nbsp;
hello but there's no green arrow on the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.160
that's because pycharm knows the signal if name&nbsp;
main means this is something that i want to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.600
no if name main means this is probably a library&nbsp;
you're not meant to just run it but why should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.680
you extract main out into a function instead&nbsp;
of just leaving the contents in the if block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.960
here's a script that just defines some function&nbsp;
and then uses it to compute something even if i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.960
put this code inside a name guard everything&nbsp;
that i do here is still in the global scope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.480
in particular this variable i is a global variable&nbsp;
even if i didn't really mean it to be that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.760
suppose i define a function to compute some value&nbsp;
and then later on i use it here i'm just trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.600
add up some evaluations of this function based off&nbsp;
of this list but i have accidentally made a typo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.680
i use the variable i here but the&nbsp;
name of the loop variable was n&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.760
when i run the code instead of&nbsp;
getting an error for my typo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.240
i silently get the wrong value this is one&nbsp;
of the worst kind of errors that you can have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.600
and this is all just within the same file&nbsp;
imagine if this happened in a different file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:36.160
if in another file or at the console i do an&nbsp;
import star of the bad script i now have a global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.000
variable i that i didn't know about so if it was&nbsp;
this file that defined the compute val function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.120
this file now has a global variable i and it's&nbsp;
even harder than in the last example to find this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:53.600
typo also in the case where you didn't have an if&nbsp;
name main at all just importing the script will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:58.800
actually run it that basically means that it's&nbsp;
impossible to test your code without side effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.800
also note that there are plenty of cases where&nbsp;
importing your code happens automatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:11.040
this script explicitly imports the useful class&nbsp;
from the bad script and then writes it to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.000
pickle file it's clear here that we're importing&nbsp;
the bad script because we explicitly wrote it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.120
but in this script there's no mention of the&nbsp;
bad script all we're doing is unpickling some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.320
object we don't even know where it came from and&nbsp;
yet when we run this code we see that we did in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:32.960
fact import the bad script and run all of the code&nbsp;
that was in its body this is because pickle.load&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:38.160
automatically imported the bad script in order&nbsp;
to get the definition of the useful class in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.800
pickle case it was just a little bit annoying to&nbsp;
have that extra stuff printed out but in this case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.400
we're doing some multi-processing the goal is to&nbsp;
compute the values of this useful function over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.840
some list of inputs multiprocessing pool is going&nbsp;
to start up a bunch of different python processes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:59.520
that all automatically import this file but&nbsp;
without an if name main you can see how this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:05.120
starts to get into trouble just importing the&nbsp;
file starts up new processes which then import&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:10.640
the file and so on so you pretty much can't use&nbsp;
multi-processing in this case look what happens

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:16.400
and i can't even kill it with&nbsp;
control c i have to kill the terminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:24.080
putting this code in an if name main prevents&nbsp;
the rampant process spawning but again without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.080
putting this in a separate function this is&nbsp;
now going to give me a global variable named p&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.680
all you have to do to avoid this headache is&nbsp;
just use the idiom it's not that much typing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.440
and in fact you could probably just set up&nbsp;
your editor to have it there automatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.680
when you make a new file or just have a&nbsp;
template to paste it when you type main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.960
one final less used reason&nbsp;
for making a main function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:52.240
is that this allows you to have an entry point to&nbsp;
your program that you can use from other scripts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:57.440
so i don't have to run this from the command line&nbsp;
i could import this from another file and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.840
call the main function as if i was running it from&nbsp;
the command line without starting a new process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:06.160
in this case you can have your main take in&nbsp;
whatever arguments as an optional parameter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.400
alright that's all i've got on the def main if&nbsp;
name main idiom let me know if you found any&nbsp;&nbsp;

